== K.1 Chapter 1 — National Income

+ *“GNP is GDP plus exports.”* It works because exports are the visible foreign item. Correction: GNP = GDP + *net factor income from abroad*; the trade balance is a different adjustment altogether.
+ *“Real GDP is nominal GDP times the price index.”* Correction: *divide*. A nominal figure at an index of 125 is larger than the real one, not smaller.
+ *“Per capita income is national income divided by the labour force.”* Correction: by the *population*. Dividing by the labour force gives output per worker, which is productivity.
+ *“Depreciation is a fall in the exchange rate.”* Correction: in the national accounts, depreciation is *capital consumed*; the currency sense is a different word used on another page.
+ *“The multiplier is the marginal propensity to consume.”* Correction: it is 1 ÷ (1 − MPC). With MPC 0.8 the multiplier is 5, not 0.8.
+ *“A transfer payment is part of national income because someone receives it.”* Correction: nothing is produced, so it is excluded. Someone receives it only because someone else's income is moved.

== K.2 Chapter 2 — Money and Banking

+ *“The primary function of money is a store of value.”* Correction: the primary function is as a *medium of exchange*; storing value is secondary and is exactly what inflation damages.
+ *“Banks create credit by printing notes.”* Correction: only the central bank issues currency. Banks create *deposits* by lending.
+ *“The credit multiplier is the reserve ratio.”* Correction: it is 1 ÷ the ratio. At 20 per cent the multiplier is 5, not 0.20.
+ *“Raising the cash reserve ratio expands credit.”* Correction: it immobilises deposits, so credit *contracts*. Every instrument has one direction.
+ *“Selling securities in the open market expands the money supply.”* Correction: buyers pay with deposits, so reserves *fall*. Buying expands.
+ *“Demand-pull and cost-push are the same thing seen from two sides.”* Correction: one is a shift of demand, the other a shift of costs, and the cures differ — reflation works for one and makes the other worse.

== K.3 Chapter 3 — Public Finance and Taxation

+ *“A budget deficit is the public debt.”* Correction: the deficit is this year's gap; the debt is the accumulated total of past gaps.
+ *“A tax on producers is borne by producers.”* Correction: statutory incidence is not economic incidence. The burden follows elasticity.
+ *“Progressive means the rich pay more naira.”* Correction: it means they pay a larger *proportion*. In a proportional tax the rich also pay more naira.
+ *“Raising the rate always raises revenue.”* Correction: beyond the peak of the Laffer curve the base shrinks faster than the rate rises.
+ *“Borrowing from the central bank is the same as borrowing from the public.”* Correction: central-bank borrowing creates money and is inflationary; borrowing from the public transfers existing savings and can crowd out investment.
+ *“A surplus means the government has no debt.”* Correction: a surplus is a flow; the debt is a stock and may still be large.

== K.4 Chapter 4 — International Trade and Payments

+ *“Absolute advantage decides the direction of trade.”* Correction: *comparative* advantage does. A country can export what it makes least badly.
+ *“A tariff raises revenue and therefore benefits the country.”* Correction: the gain to the treasury and to producers is smaller than the loss to consumers; the net is a deadweight loss.
+ *“A quota is a tax.”* Correction: it is a quantity limit. It raises the price like a tax but yields no revenue to the treasury.
+ *“Devaluation always improves the trade balance.”* Correction: only when demand is elastic enough. With inelastic demand the balance worsens first — the J-curve.
+ *“The balance of payments can be in deficit, so the accounts do not balance.”* Correction: the accounts always balance; the deficit is on one account and is financed by the other or by reserves.
+ *“The terms of trade improve when export volume rises.”* Correction: the terms of trade are a *price ratio*. Volume does not enter them.

== K.5 Chapter 5 — Development, Population and Unemployment

+ *“Growth and development are the same.”* Correction: growth is real output; development includes health, schooling and distribution. Growth without development is the paper's favourite example.
+ *“A high HDI means a high income.”* Correction: the index combines income with health and schooling; a country can have income without either.
+ *“Unemployment is a share of the population.”* Correction: of the *labour force*. The population share is a different, smaller number.
+ *“A falling unemployment rate always means the labour market is improving.”* Correction: discouraged workers leaving the labour force lower the rate while the market worsens. Check the participation rate.
+ *“Structural unemployment is cured by spending.”* Correction: by retraining and mobility. Demand measures leave the mismatch in place.
+ *“A high dependency ratio means a high birth rate.”* Correction: it may be the elderly, or children, or both. The ratio says how many are supported, not why.

== K.6 Chapter 6 — The Nigerian Economy

+ *“The CBN publishes GDP.”* Correction: the National Bureau of Statistics does. The CBN publishes money, reserves and the external accounts.
+ *“The CBN collects company income tax.”* Correction: the Federal Inland Revenue Service does.
+ *“Oil employs most Nigerians.”* Correction: agriculture does. Oil earns the foreign exchange and employs comparatively few.
+ *“Derivation benefits the largest states.”* Correction: it benefits the states where the revenue was generated — the oil-producing states.
+ *“The naira's external value rises when demand for dollars rises.”* Correction: it *falls*, because more naira are needed to buy the scarcer dollars.
+ *“An inflation rate is the change in the index since last month.”* Correction: it is the change over twelve months unless the question says otherwise.

#v(5pt)

== K.7 The Last Twenty-Four Hours

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  + *Read four pages, not four chapters.* Appendices D, F and K are written for this evening; a new topic on the last night buys nothing and costs sleep.
  + *Reread the wrong-answer log.* It is the only revision written in your own words, and it is the one that fits in twenty minutes.
  + *Do ten easy questions.* Ten correct answers in fifteen minutes rebuild the speed and confidence a hard paper erodes.
  + *Set out your materials the night before:* admission slip, pencil, pen, water, and the route to the centre.
  + *Sleep.* A candidate who has slept remembers more of what was revised than one who has revised twice as much and slept half as long.
]

#v(5pt)

== K.8 The First Five Minutes

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  + *Write the four formulas at the top of the rough sheet:* real = nominal ÷ index, the multiplier, the credit multiplier and unemployment ÷ labour force. They are the arithmetic the paper will ask for.
  + *Scan the paper for the two or three questions you can answer instantly.* Answering them first converts nervous energy into marks.
  + *Check the number of questions and the time.* Forty questions in thirty minutes is forty-five seconds each, and no question is worth two minutes.
  + *Breathe out once, slowly, before the first question.* The first ten seconds set the pace for the first ten questions.
]

#v(5pt)

