== K.10 The Calculation Routine

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  + *Write the formula before you touch the numbers.* Half the wrong answers in the paper come from a half-remembered formula, not from bad arithmetic.
  + *Substitute in one line, compute in the next.* Long working is where signs are lost.
  + *Carry the unit to the answer.* Naira, per cent, or a bare ratio. An elasticity has no unit at all.
  + *Check against the options before choosing.* If your answer is not listed, the slip is arithmetic, and it is cheaper to recompute than to guess.
]

#v(4pt)

#text(font: "DejaVu Sans", size: 8.6pt)[Guessing, properly: eliminate the option that reverses the direction, then the one that uses the wrong base, then choose between what remains. An answer that cannot be wrong for either reason is right more often than one in four.]

#v(5pt)

== K.11 The Sixty Seconds After

#block(width: 100%, fill: white, stroke: 0.8pt + jamb-border, radius: 7pt, inset: 10pt)[
  + *Check the count.* Forty answers for forty questions; a blank box is a mark thrown away.
  + *Check that no two answers are in the same box.* A slipped row is the most expensive error in a CBT paper.
  + *Do not change an answer without a reason.* First instincts are right more often than last-minute doubt, and the doubt has no formula behind it.
  + *Write down the three questions you were unsure of.* They become the first entries in tomorrow's wrong-answer log.
]
